column2:
Why did you fasten on my s[on] Gilgameš a heart that cannot sleep — why afflict him?
47	Now you have touched him, and he goes
48	the long road to the place of Humbaba;
49	he will face a combat he has not known;
50	he will ride a campaign he has not known,
51	all the days of his going and returning,
52	until he arrives at the Cedar Forest,
53	until he strikes down ferocious Humbaba
54	and roots out of the land the evil thing you hate.
55	Each day, when you [ ... ] the day's bound,
56	may she — may Ayya the Bride — unafraid, remind you:
57	'Commit him to the watches of the night!'
58	[In the ev]ening watch [ ... ]
62	[ ... ] of the brightening.
63	You opened, Š[amaš, the gate for the herds] to go out;
64	to [ ... ] you came forth over the land.
65	The uplands [ ... ] the heavens;
66	the beasts of the steppe [ ... ] your red glow;
67	[ ... ] waits f[or ... ] them;
68	the animals [ ... ] you.
69	[ ... ] carries to you;
70	the dead [ ... li]fe;
71	to [ ... ] your head;
72	at the ris[ing of ... the peo]ples gather;
73	the Anunn[aki] attend [on your light(?).]
74	May she — [may Ayya the Bride,] unafraid, [remind you:]
75	'Commit him t[o the watches of the n]ight!'
76	Set a road o[f safety(?)] und[er his feet;]
77	appoint [Enk]idu [to go] before him,
78	since he kno[ws] the road of the Ce[dar] Forest —
79	[let him lead(?)] the road for him; [let him choose the passes(?)] of the mountains;
80	and [let him carry] the tor[ch(?), the si]gn of the road, in his hands.
81	Until Gilgameš arrives at the Ce[dar] Forest,
82	let the days be long; let the nights be short;
83	let his loins be girded; let [his str]ide be [sure;]
84	at nightfall let him pitch a camp for the night,
85	and at night [ ... ] let them lie down to rest.
86	May she — may Ayya the Bride, the great one — unafraid, remind you.
87	On the day Gilgameš, Enkidu, and Humbaba close with one another,
88	raise against Humbaba, Šamaš, the great gale-winds:
89	South Wind, North Wind, East Wind, West Wind, Blast, Counterblast,
90	Typhoon, Evil Wind, Simurru-wind, Demon-wind,
91	Frost-wind, Storm, Whirlwind —
92	let the thirteen winds rise so the face of Humbaba is darkened
93	and the weapon of Gilgameš reaches Humbaba!
94	Once your own fires are kindled(?),
95	in that hour, Šamaš, turn your face to the one who prays to you;
96	let your swift mules ca[rr]y you onward;
97	a restful seat, a couch of the night, will be laid for you;
97a	a seat [ ... ]
98	the gods your brothers will bring you food-offerings of [ ... ];
99	and Ayya the Bride, with the clean hem of her garment, will wipe your face.

Mighty Enkidu, you are no issue of my womb;
122	but now I adopt you, with the oblates of Gilgameš,
123	the priestesses, the hierodules, the temple-women.

The priestesses take in the foundling,
126	and the daughters of the gods raise up the ward.
127	I — I take Enkidu, wh[om I love,] for my son;
128	Enkidu, as a b[rother(?),] let Gi[lg]ameš tre[at] him well!
131	Whi[le you g]o [with him ... ] to the Cedar Forest,
132	let [the days] be lo[ng;] let the nights be short;
133	[let your loins be girded; let] your [str]ide be [sure;]
134	[at nightfall pitch a camp for the ni]ght;
135	[ ... let him(?)] protect [ ... ]
